[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering UIKit Lifecycle, UIViewController Containment, and Custom View Hierarchies in iOS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern iOS development, mastering how view controllers transition through their lifecycle, how to compose multiple view controllers using containment, and how to structure custom view hierarchies is essential for building scalable and memory-efficient applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding the UIViewController Lifecycle"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A view controller manages a view hierarchy. Knowing the exact sequence of lifecycle methods ensures that you initialize data, subscribe to notifications, and release resources at the correct moments."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"loadView(): Creates the view controller's view programmatically. Never call this method directly."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"viewDidLoad(): Called after the view has been loaded. Ideal for one-time setup like constraint configurations and data bindings."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"viewWillAppear(): Called just before the view appears on screen. Use for refreshing data or starting network requests."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"viewDidAppear(): Called when the view is fully visible. Ideal for starting animations or analytics tracking."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"viewWillDisappear() and viewDidDisappear(): Called when the view is being covered or removed. Use for stopping timers, saving state, or removing observers."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. UIViewController Containment"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"UIViewController containment allows you to combine the output of multiple view controllers into a single unified interface. Standard UIKit examples include UINavigationController and UITabBarController. You can also build custom container view controllers like dashboards or master-detail layouts."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When adding a child view controller, you must explicitly forward lifecycle events to ensure that viewWillAppear and other methods are called correctly on the child."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"func displayChildController(child: UIViewController, on containerView: UIView) {
    addChild(child)
    child.view.frame = containerView.bounds
    containerView.addSubview(child.view)
    child.didMove(toParent: self)
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Similarly, removing a child requires a strict cleanup process to prevent memory leaks and broken lifecycle states:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"func removeChildController(child: UIViewController) {
    child.willMove(toParent: nil)
    child.view.removeFromSuperview()
    child.removeFromParent()
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Custom View Hierarchies"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"A robust custom view hierarchy separates layout logic from view controllers. By subclassing UIView and overriding layoutSubviews or configuring Auto Layout internally, you encapsulate UI components cleanly."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"class ProfileCardView: UIView {
    private let titleLabel = UILabel()
    private let avatarImageView = UIImageView()

    override init(frame: CGRect) {
        super.init(frame: frame)
        setupHierarchy()
        setupConstraints()
    }

    required init?(coder: NSCoder) {
        fatalError("init(coder:) has not been implemented")
    }

    private func setupHierarchy() {
        addSubview(avatarImageView)
        addSubview(titleLabel)
    }

    private func setupConstraints() {
        // Configure Auto Layout constraints here
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By properly orchestrating the view controller lifecycle, strictly adhering to containment rules, and structuring clean custom view hierarchies, you will build highly performant, maintainable, and modular iOS applications."}]
